eys. Actually INSTANT might need no extra props. Let me try transition:{type:'INSTANT'}.\n\nWait but for AFTER_TIMEOUT the trigger shape {type:'AFTER_TIMEOUT', timeout:2000} — validation said \"Required value missing at [0].trigger.timeout\" as one alternative... that alternative appears when type isn't matched? The message: \"Expected [0].trigger to be one of the following... Invalid enum value Expected 'ON_CLICK'... received 'ON_TAP' at [0].trigger.type\" — this branch corresponds to MOUSE-enter etc. And \"Invalid literal value, expected 'AFTER_TIMEOUT' ... Required value missing at [0].trigger.timeout\" — hmm, it says for trigger[0] expected AFTER_TIMEOUT with timeout missing. But I DID pass timeout:2000 in the first reaction... unless the error is on reaction [0] of the SECOND assignment: splash.reactions=[...splash.reactions, {ON_TAP...}] — the first assignment failed before, so splash.reactions never got set; second assignment's reactions=[...old, onTap]. Old reactions were [] maybe. The error shows [0] failing — with ON_TAP trigger and NODE action without transition — that's my second assignment's tap reaction. So [0] = tap reaction: trigger ON_TAP invalid → should be ON_CLICK; action NODE missing transition.\n\nSo the timeout reaction might actually be fine, but since I combined both in one call and the second assignment threw... wait, no — the FIRST assignment (timeout) would have run first. If it succeeded, splash.reactions would contain it, and error would be about [1]. Error is about [0], and mentions ON_TAP — so the first assignment failed?? The first assignment action = {type:'NODE',destinationId,navigation:'NAVIGATE'} — no transition → invalid. So assignment 1 threw (the error we see), and assignment 2 never ran. The error message is from assignment 1's trigger: AFTER_TIMEOUT with timeout... wait but error says trigger expected ON_CLICK etc. and also lists \"Invalid literal value, expected 'AFTER_TIMEOUT' at [0].trigger.type, Required value missing at